   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to extending the authority of the department of environmental conservation to manage Atlantic and shortnose sturgeon",
    "basePrintNo": "S6483",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6483-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0339-a, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6483\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the environmental conservation law,\nin relation to extending the authority of the department of\nenvironmental conservation to manage Atlantic and shortnose sturgeon\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend the authority of the Department\nof Environmental Conservation (DEC) to manage Atlantic and shortnose\nsturgeon.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of this bill would amend Environmental Conservation Law\n(ECL) § 13-0339-a(1)(c) to extend, until December 31, 2020, DEC's\nauthority to fix by regulation measures for the management of Atlantic\nsturgeon (Acipenser oxyrhynchus) and shortnose sturgeon (Acipenser\nbrevirostrum).\n\nSection 2 of this bill would make it effective immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nDEC's regulatory authority for Atlantic and shortnose sturgeon is\nnecessary to preserve the current regulatory program. Failure to\nextend the existing regulatory authority by December 31, 2017 will\nresult in the loss of regulatory protection for this species.\n\nThis bill would extend DEC's regulatory authority for Atlantic and\nshortnose sturgeon for an additional three years, and ensure that DEC\ncontinues to maintain compliance with any applicable federal\nmanagement plan or Interstate Fishery. Management Plan (FMP) adopted\nby the Atlantic States Marine Fisheries Commission (ASMFC). ECL §\n13-0105, which sets forth the marine fisheries conservation and\nmanagement policy, provides that one of the State's objectives shall\nbe that the management of the State's transboundary and migratory\nspecies shall be consistent with any interjurisdictional management\nplans, interstate or state-federal. Failure to make necessary\nregulatory changes as may be required by an applicable FMP could\nresult in the closure of New York's fishery for that species.\n\nAtlantic sturgeon is managed pursuant to the Interstate Fishery\nManagement Plan for Atlantic Sturgeon adopted by ASMFC. The 1998\namendment to the FMP required closure of all states' sturgeon\nfisheries and the possession of Atlantic sturgeon is prohibited in New\nYork. The species was listed as \"endangered\" in April 2012, pursuant\nto the federal Endangered Species Act of 1973. The shortnose sturgeon\nis listed as endangered, and is therefore also fully protected by the\nEndangered Species Act of 1973. Abundance of both sturgeon species is\nincreasing, and their status may be upgraded in the future. If so, it\nwill be necessary for New York to continue to have regulatory\nauthority to protect the recovered stocks.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nDEC's regulatory authority was last extended by Chapter 133 of the\nLaws of 2015.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
